\section{Relativistic Jet Physics}
The relativistic mass flux required to sustain a fixed power output $P$ is
\begin{equation}
\dot{m} = \frac{P}{(\gamma - 1)\eta c^2},
\end{equation}
where $\eta = 0.95$ is the conversion efficiency.  
At $\gamma = 10.015$, the Mk-30 mass flux is
\[
\dot{m} = 12.98~\text{g/s}.
\]

The relativistic thrust is
\begin{equation}
F_{\text{jet}} = \gamma \dot{m} v,
\end{equation}
yielding $F_{\text{jet}} \approx 38.48~\text{MN}$.

\section{Magnetohydrodynamic Collimation}
The jet collimation half-angle is
\begin{equation}
\theta_{\text{open}} \approx \frac{1}{\gamma},
\end{equation}
giving $\theta_{\text{open}} \approx 5.72^\circ$.

The Poynting flux ratio is
\begin{equation}
\sigma = \frac{B^2}{4\pi \gamma \rho c^2},
\end{equation}
which decreases from $\sigma = 15.2$ at the nozzle core to $\sigma = 0.1$ at 
jet termination.

